## What we are actually protecting when we say "UNESCO"

On 24 July 2021 Mariánské Lázně (Marienbad) was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List as one of the eleven components of the property **The Great Spa Towns of Europe**, spread across seven countries. The component covers 835 hectares and includes not only the historic centre with its springs, colonnades and spa houses, but also the surrounding therapeutic landscape: the parks, the forest parks with paths up to Hamelika, and the golf course on the eastern edge of the town. Around it lies a buffer zone of 3,677 hectares, which follows the town's boundaries and protects the woods of the Slavkov Forest.

That is exactly why it makes sense to get to know the town on foot. The following circuit begins and ends at the Main Colonnade and, at an unhurried pace, takes a morning — roughly 2–3 hours including stops at the springs; the lookout tower or the golf course will add an afternoon. The background to the inscription is explained on the [UNESCO](/en/unesco) page and in the article [Why Marienbad is on the UNESCO list](/en/magazine/why-marienbad-unesco); this text is about where to walk.

## The route: eight stops around the spa centre

### 1. The Main Colonnade

Start in the middle of the cast-iron promenade, which is a National Heritage Monument. The neo-Baroque colonnade was built in 1888–1889 to designs by the Viennese architects Hans Miksch and Julian Niedzielski, and its cast-iron structure was made by the Blansko ironworks. Today it measures 135 metres (originally 180 metres together with the so-called Old Colonnade), and the nomination dossier describes it as the longest colonnade in a spa town. Look up: the ceiling is covered by Josef Vyleťal's fresco cycle on the theme "Man and the Universe". Details are on the [Colonnade and Singing Fountain](/en/colonnade) page.

{% figure
   src="/images/library/colonnade/main-colonnade-16a4667.jpg"
   alt="The cast-iron Main Colonnade in Marienbad with its painted ceiling and promenading guests"
   caption="The Main Colonnade of 1888–1889: 135 metres of covered promenade and the starting point of the circuit."
   width="wide" /%}

### 2. The Singing Fountain

Walk a few steps down in front of the colonnade to the circular basin, 18 metres in diameter. The fountain was built in 1982–1986 to a design by the architect Pavel Mikšík and first played on 30 April 1986 with Petr Hapka's composition "Music for a Fountain". It plays every odd hour from 30 April to 31 October; the best view is from the colonnade steps, and in the evening it is illuminated as well.

{% figure
   src="/images/library/colonnade/singing-fountain-16a3648.jpg"
   alt="The Singing Fountain in front of the colonnade with its jets of water and the Cross Spring pavilion in the background"
   caption="The fountain plays every odd hour; the season runs from 30 April to 31 October."
   width="wide" /%}

### 3. The Cross Spring pavilion

At the end of the colonnade stands the domed pavilion, the symbol of Marienbad. The Cross Spring is first mentioned in the annals of the Teplá monastery in 1749 as a "salty acidulous water", and the same year the pharmacist Damián Schulz erected a wooden cross beside it, from which the spring took its name. The image of the Virgin Mary hung by the spring then gave the whole place its name in 1808. The colonnade temple above the spring was built in 1818–1826, probably to designs by Georg Fischer. The Rudolph and Caroline Springs also rise by the colonnade; all the springs here are cold acidulous waters at 7–10 °C, each with its own taste. The character of the springs is described in the [springs overview](/en/springs-overview); drinking as part of a cure is decided by the spa physician — see [mineral springs](/en/mineral-springs).

{% figure
   src="/images/library/colonnade/cross-spring-pavilion-16a1598.jpg"
   alt="The neoclassical Cross Spring pavilion with its dome and colonnade"
   caption="The Cross Spring pavilion of 1818–1826 adjoins the colonnade directly."
   width="wide" /%}

### 4. Skalník's Gardens and the spa park

From the pavilion, return along the colonnade and descend into the park. The gardener Václav Skalník, later mayor of the town, began transforming the marshy valley into a park town in 1817; with the architect Georg Fischer and the builder Anton Thurner, the basis of today's centre was created in 1817–1823, later remodelled in the English landscape style. Keep to the main path down through the park, past the cast-iron bandstand and, in May, the flowering rhododendrons. This is where you can see why UNESCO values the interconnection of springs, buildings and greenery rather than individual structures. The continuation into the forests is described on the [Nature and parks](/en/nature) page.

{% figure
   src="/images/library/colonnade/park-16a4873.jpg"
   alt="A path through the spa park with mature trees and flower beds below the colonnade"
   caption="Skalník's Gardens: the park that has linked the colonnade with the spa houses since 1817."
   width="wide" /%}

### 5. The Ambrose Spring and the Central Baths

In the lower part of the park, close to the Central Baths, the Ambrose Spring rises from three outlets — a plain iron-rich acidulous water with a rusty trace at the outlet. The Central Baths with their domed tower are among the historic spa houses within the component that still serve their original purpose today. From the spring, continue to the neighbouring Nové Lázně.

### 6. Nové Lázně and the Roman Baths

Nové Lázně, a building by Marienbad native Josef Schaffer in Italian Neo-Renaissance style from 1893–1896, was ceremonially opened on 1 June 1896. On the ground floor the Roman Baths survive in the form of an ancient double atrium with marble columns, together with two historic cabins for carbonated baths, today called the Imperial and Royal cabins. The latter commemorates King Edward VII of Britain, who visited Marienbad nine times in 1897–1909. The building now serves as a spa hotel; the historic interiors are open to guests. More on the [Architecture](/en/architecture) page.

### 7. Goethe Square

From Nové Lázně, climb up to Goethe Square above the spa park. It is dominated by the deanery Church of the Assumption of the Virgin Mary of 1844–1848, built in the neo-Byzantine style to plans by the Munich architect Johann Gottfried Gutensohn. Also on the square stands the Golden Grape House of 1818, the oldest surviving neoclassical house in the town, where Johann Wolfgang von Goethe stayed in 1823; today it houses the Municipal Museum with Goethe's preserved rooms. The poet visited Marienbad three times in 1820–1823, and it was his stay here that gave rise to his "Elegy".

### 8. Back to the colonnade along Hlavní třída

Descend to Hlavní třída (Main Street) and walk past the Chopin House (Hlavní 47), where Fryderyk Chopin stayed in 1836, and past the Municipal Theatre of 1868 by Friedrich Zickler, remodelled in Art Nouveau style in 1905. The circuit closes at the Casino social house, the former Kursaal of 1867, from which the colonnade is within sight.

## Optional extension: Hamelika or golf

**The Hamelika lookout tower.** From Goethe Square, marked paths lead through the forest park, which is part of the UNESCO component, up to Hamelika hill (722 m above sea level). The 20-metre stone lookout tower with its 100 steps was built in 1876 by the builder Friedrich Zickler. The circuit there and back takes roughly an hour and a half; further routes are on the [day trips](/en/day-trips) page.

{% figure
   src="/images/trips/hamelika.jpg"
   alt="The stone Hamelika lookout tower among the trees above the spa quarter"
   caption="The Hamelika lookout tower of 1876 stands at 722 m above sea level in the forest park above the town."
   credit="Photo: Lubor Ferenc · CC BY-SA 4.0"
   creditHref="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rozhledna_Hamelika_(3).jpg"
   width="wide" /%}

**Royal Golf Club.** On the eastern edge of the component lies the course of the club founded in 1904, ceremonially opened on 21 August 1905 in the presence of King Edward VII; in 1929 it was extended to 18 holes, and since 2003 the club has been permitted to use the title "Royal". The UNESCO nomination dossier ranks it among the oldest golf courses in Central Europe. Details on the [golf](/en/golf) page.

## Practical information

**When to set off.** The town is at its liveliest from the opening of the spa season in May until October, when the Singing Fountain plays and guided forest walks take place (June to September). The International Chopin Festival, held since 1960, takes place in August, and the Marienbad Film Festival at the turn of August and September. The colonnade and parks are open all year round; in December the circuit is complemented by the Advent markets. Tips by month: [best time to visit](/en/best-time-to-visit).

**What to bring.** A spa cup or your own beaker for tasting the springs, comfortable shoes — the route runs over paving, park paths and, in the case of Hamelika, a forest trail — and an extra layer, because the spa centre lies at about 630 metres and mornings can be fresh even in summer. Afternoon coffee goes with spa wafers, which have been baked in Marienbad since 1856.

**Pace.** Do not hurry. The colonnade is at its most beautiful early in the morning, when guests gather at the springs with their cups, and in the early evening by the illuminated fountain. Anyone with more days to spare can split the circuit in two.

